                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Summary[/h1]\r\nModern submarine warfare sim with a steep learning curve that will fit for players who like a deeper naval combat experience but hard to recommend for other players.\r\n\r\nHard to rate this, for some people it may be a clear positive, but looking at it on a wider player base I think it's a competent sim but not a good game. The simulation core is good but it needed more work to make this an engaging game for the more casual player.\r\n\r\n[h1]Positive[/h1]\r\nFairly deep modern submarine and naval combat simulation with modern vessels, weapons and sonar tech.\r\n\r\nInteresting stealth mechanics - ships emit individual noise signatures that you can pick up and match on your sonar device. Detecting other ships or being detected depends on speed, depth, water layers, etc. For example go faster in shallow water and your propeller \"cavitates\" making bubbles and extra noise so you are easier to detect.\r\n\r\nVisuals for the subs and boats are quite good though you often don't get enemy visuals unless you have a  full range finding lock on them.\r\n\r\n[h1]Negative[/h1]\r\nSteep learning curve - there are some tutorials but beyond that it can be hard to pick up the uses for various weapons and the tactics to engage or evade enemies. Quite a bit of trial and error unless you look up and follow online tutorials.\r\n\r\nUnderwhelming single missions - there's a bunch single player standalone missions offered, but the game doesn't track which ones you've played and completed. So if you come back to the game after a while you don't remember which you have not completed yet. Needed some more game structure here and maybe a few more missions.\r\n\r\nSingle player campaign - adds some atmosphere with cut scenes and a backstory. Then it looks like a string of missions: somehow it doesn't pull me in and I've only done a few.\r\n\r\nRandom start positions and surface vessel speeds - for the standalone and campaign missions the vessel starting positions and the NPC vessel headings look random. Makes repeated practicing of a failed mission harder. The surface vessels can be quite fast and it can be long and tedious to track and close in on them without being detected as you must keep your own speed limited.\r\n\r\nUI - minor gripe with the on-screen controls: the same on-screen button location is used for plot course or an emergency surface action, depending on the current UI mode. A few times in a critical moment instead of changing course I've launched the boat to the surface and then the enemy has me, game over.",
